    <section id="services" data-section-key="leistungen">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Services</p>
        <h2>Two things I take off your hands.</h2>

        <div class="why">
          <h3>Ongoing IT care</h3>
          <p>I take care of what has to run every day: workstations, updates, access rights, backups. And I write down what I do.</p>
          <p>That sounds obvious, but it's exactly where most businesses come unstuck. When, after an incident, the insurer asks when a backup was last restored, or a major client demands proof of your access control, you need more than a good feeling. You need a record.</p>
          <p>With you, that runs automatically. Once a month you get an overview you can present: backup runs, patch status, open items.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="why">
          <h3>Servers, network and virtualization</h3>
          <p>When the server is getting on in years, the network has grown historically, or the VMware bill suddenly looks different than before: that's my core area. I've done Linux and virtualization for over ten years. My own production environment runs on Proxmox, with over 35 virtual machines and around 40 containers that I set up and run myself. What I recommend to you, I run myself first.</p>
          <p><strong>Leaving VMware:</strong> regular support for vSphere 8 ends on 11 October 2027. Migrating to Proxmox takes one to two years for mid-sized environments, so if you want to act in 2027, you should do the math now. I'll tell you honestly whether the switch is worth it for you or whether you're better off staying. <a class="card-link" href="/vmware-ausstieg/">Check VMware cost →</a></p>
          <p><strong>A network that separates instead of just connecting:</strong> production, office and guests don't belong on the same network, and remote access needs to be secured. That's no longer optional, it's written into the terms of every cyber-insurance policy.</p>
          <p><strong>Where manual work is unnecessary, I automate it.</strong> Reports someone pulls together from three systems every month. Approvals that run by word of mouth. Records someone compiles by hand. For that I build you a small tool, not a big project, but the solution for exactly the one workflow that's costing you time. <a class="card-link" href="/en/demos/">See examples as a live demo →</a></p>
        </div>

        <p class="trust-bar">Monthly packages for ongoing care · Fixed prices for scoped projects · Documentation that stays with you</p>
      </div>
    </section>

